WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.000
In these next few videos,

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.880
I'm going to be covering cholesterol-
lowering drugs like statins,

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.280
why LDL cholesterol
is such a major threat,

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:12.320
for whom the benefits of
these drugs outweigh the risks,

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.255
and what we can do to
lower our cholesterol naturally.

00:00:15.255 --> 00:00:21.929
"Why Isn't Everyone on Cholesterol-
Lowering Statin Drugs?"

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:26.000
LDL cholesterol,
also known as bad cholesterol,

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.920
is unequivocally recognized
as the principal driving force

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.120
in the development

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:34.080
of atherosclerotic
cardiovascular disease,

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.400
our leading cause of death.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.440
Over the decades, we have seen
the guidelines shift

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.120
to lower and lower LDL targets

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.200
on the basis of clinical trials

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.320
demonstrating that lower
is better,

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:47.520
starting in the 1980s

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.920
with an LDL target of 130,

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.400
then down to a hundred
in the ’90s,

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.080
and maybe down to 70
for those at really high risk,

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:59.120
and then maybe even down
to 55, 40, or 30

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.040
over the last decade.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.080
Those more recent targets
might actually be closer

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.800
to normal for the human species.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.160
Even after we learned
to use tools so we could hunt,

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.240
normal LDL has been
in the 50 to 70 range,

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.240
but today, the average
in the Western world

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.480
is more like 120 mg/dL.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:23.520
No wonder heart disease
is our leading cause of death

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.360
in men and women.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.400
Recently, guidelines
started scrapping targets

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.200
in favor of just pushing
for LDL levels

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.520
to be as low as possible,

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:35.920
because the lower the better.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.000
No threshold
seems to exist below

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.480
which LDL cholesterol-lowering
does not further reduce risk.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.320
When it comes to LDL,
it’s possible

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:47.200
that lower is better
for longer,

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:51.040
even if you start out
at low risk.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.520
The risk reduction
of major vascular events

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.720
is independent
of the starting LDL cholesterol,

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.400
meaning that even people
starting out

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.320
with an LDL under 80

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:03.280
had about the same relative
risk reduction.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:06.560
So, even if your LDL
is quote-unquote "normal,"

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:08.880
even if other heart disease
risk factors

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.040
are considered optimal,

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.840
it’s considered
of utmost importance

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:15.280
to control it.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:17.840
So, why not just put
cholesterol-lowering drugs

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:19.920
like statins
in the drinking water

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.840
like we do fluoride?

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.440
Why aren’t statins
prescribed for everyone?

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.520
Because of the downsides.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:29.040
There’s the risk
of side effects,

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.520
plus the burden of having
to take a pill every day

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.120
for the rest of your life.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.360
So, that’s why these drugs
are only recommended for people

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:38.080
at relatively high risk
of having a heart attack

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.040
for whom the pros
of cholesterol-lowering

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.160
outweigh the cons
of taking the drug.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.080
Okay, but when it comes to LDL,

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.080
if "lower is better for longer,

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:50.080
and the earlier the better,"

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.160
and the only reason
we’re not giving more drugs

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:54.320
is the downsides,

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.320
what if there were
safe, simple,

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:59.280
side-effect-free solutions
to lowering our cholesterol,

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:00.880
for example,
eating specific types

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.040
of healthy foods every day?

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.520
Well, in that case, regardless
if we’re on drugs or not,

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:08.480
shouldn’t we utilize

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:11.120
every safe no-downside strategy
there is

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:14.480
to get our LDL
down as low as possible?

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.560
Before I get
to all those strategies,

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.160
let’s answer
the on-drugs-or-not question.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:21.840
According to the latest
cholesterol

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.440
clinical practice guidelines

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:24.600
from the American
Heart Association

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.200
and the American College
of Cardiology,

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:29.520
the number one
take-home message

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:33.360
is a lifelong
heart-healthy lifestyle.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:37.040
Okay, but when do
they also recommend drugs?

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:39.120
If you have been diagnosed
with heart disease,

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.560
like if you already
had a heart attack

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.160
and are trying
to prevent another one,

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:45.360
then drugs are considered
non-negotiable.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.680
Okay, but what about
primary prevention,

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:50.320
meaning preventing
that first heart attack?

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:53.760
Well, if you have an LDL
of 190 or more,

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.800
then "don’t pass go,
don’t collect $200,"

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.200
go straight to statins.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.120
Similarly, if you’re between 40
and 75 and have diabetes,

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.040
then an LDL of even 70

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.200
gets you a statin
recommendation.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.080
Now, if you don’t have
known cardiovascular disease

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:11.120
or diabetes,

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:14.480
and your LDL is between 70
and 190,

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:17.040
then statins are
generally recommended

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.000
if your risk of having
a cardiovascular event,

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.440
like a heart attack or stroke,

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:26.400
is 7.5% or more
over the next ten years.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.280
Here’s the flow chart
for primary prevention,

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.480
based on age, LDL,
diabetes status,

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:33.760
and ten-year risk.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.640
You can pause
and work your way through it.

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:42.080
As you can see, even at
a ten-year risk as low as 5%,

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:43.360
a statin may be recommended

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:45.600
if you have
risk-enhancing factors

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:49.520
such as high triglycerides
or a bad family history.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.800
And if you’re in that
intermediate risk category

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.080
where statins are recommended,
but you’re still on the fence,

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:58.640
a coronary calcium scan
imaging test

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.600
can be done to help you decide.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.520
How do you figure out
what your ten-year risk is?

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.000
In a previous video
I did on the topic,

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:11.280
I recommended three
common risk calculators,

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.760
but I have a new favorite,
u-prevent.com.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:17.675
That’s just the letter U, dash,

00:05:17.675 --> 00:05:20.560
prevent (P-R-E-V-E-N-T)
dot com.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:21.840
It’s free and endorsed

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:24.160
by the European Society
of Cardiology.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:27.040
Not only does it give you
a 10-year risk estimate,

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:29.520
and a lifetime risk estimate,

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:33.200
but it estimates how long
you may have before the event

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.520
and, best of all,
you can then toggle

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:37.040
various treatment options

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:39.920
to see what may happen
to your risk if you follow them,

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:41.680
like what happens
if you stop smoking,

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:45.280
or increase your step count,
or start a statin.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:50.160
If this woman does nothing,
then her 8.6% ten-year risk

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.480
would likely merit a statin,

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:55.520
but whether or not the overall
benefit-harm balance

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:57.120
justifies the use
of a medication

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:58.880
for an individual patient

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:02.320
cannot be determined
by a guidelines committee,

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:05.680
a healthcare system,
or even the attending physician.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:08.960
Instead, it is
the individual patient

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:10.800
who has a fundamental right
to decide

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.200
whether or not to take a drug
is worthwhile.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:15.040
It’s your body, your choice;

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:17.680
so, let’s go through the pros
and cons

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:22.120
so you can decide
for yourself, next.

